<script lang="ts">
	import { englishExamples as examples } from '../../utils/examples.en'

	const open = signal(false)
	const mode = signal<'lucide:moon' | 'lucide:sun'>('lucide:sun')
	const toggleMode = (): void => {
		mode.value = mode.value === 'lucide:sun' ? 'lucide:moon' : 'lucide:sun'
	}
</script>

<template>
	<DocsPage
		locale="en"
		title="Icon"
		description="Iconify icons and your own SVGs, typed and inlined in the HTML at build time."
	>
		<h2 id="usage">Usage</h2>
		<DocsPreview>
			<p>
				<UIIcon name="lucide:search" />
				Search the project
			</p>
			<UIButton variant="outline">
				<UIIcon
					name="lucide:trash-2"
					label="Delete"
				/>
			</UIButton>
		</DocsPreview>
		<DocsCode bind:code="examples.icon" />
		<p>
			<code>UIIcon</code>
			writes an inline
			<code>&lt;svg&gt;</code>. Each literal name is resolved during the build with the collection package installed in the project: there are no runtime requests or CDN, and the bundle only carries the icons you use.
		</p>

		<h2 id="setup">Installation and collections</h2>
		<p>
			You need
			<code>@meekojs/meeko</code>
			<code>&gt;=1.8 &lt;2</code>, with modules registered by name. Install the module and each Iconify collection you want to use as
			<code>@iconify-json/&lt;collection&gt;</code>. The module is registered by its name and its options go in the
			<code>icon</code>
			key, typed in
			<code>meeko.config.ts</code>.
		</p>
		<DocsCode bind:code="examples.iconConfig" />
		<p>
			The available names form a TypeScript union that Meeko generates in
			<code>.meeko/types</code>: the editor completes
			<code>name="lucide:…"</code>, and a name that does not exist is a
			<code>meeko check</code>
			error with the closest suggestion. If
			<code>icon.collections</code>
			names a collection that is not installed,
			<code>meeko check</code>,
			<code>meeko dev</code>
			and
			<code>meeko build</code>
			fail with the command to install it.
		</p>

		<h2 id="dynamic">Dynamic names</h2>
		<p>
			With
			<code>bind:name</code>, the literals of the expression are resolved at build time just like a fixed name. A value that does not appear in the code, such as one that comes from data, only works if it is in
			<code>icon.include</code>
			(or with
			<code>'collection:*'</code>, which includes the whole collection). Otherwise an empty
			<code>&lt;svg&gt;</code>
			is rendered and
			<code>meeko dev</code>
			warns in the console.
		</p>
		<DocsPreview>
			<UIButton
				variant="secondary"
				on:click="() => (open.value = !open.value)"
			>
				<UIIcon
					bind:name="open.value ? 'lucide:x' : 'lucide:menu'"
					bind:label="open.value ? 'Close menu' : 'Open menu'"
				/>
			</UIButton>
			<UIButton
				variant="secondary"
				on:click="toggleMode"
			>
				<UIIcon
					bind:name="mode.value"
					bind:label="mode.value === 'lucide:sun' ? 'Light mode' : 'Dark mode'"
				/>
			</UIButton>
		</DocsPreview>
		<DocsCode bind:code="examples.iconDynamic" />

		<h2 id="resolution">How it is resolved</h2>
		<ul>
			<li
				><strong>Literal names.</strong>
				During the build, a Vite plugin resolves each
				<code>name="…"</code>
				of
				<code>&lt;UIIcon&gt;</code>
				with
				<code>@iconify/utils</code>
				and inlines it in the component that uses it, before Meeko compiles it. The bundle of each page only carries its icons; SSR and the browser see the same SVG, so hydration and navigation do not flicker.</li
			>
			<li
				><strong>A name that does not exist.</strong>
				It is a type error (<code>TS2820</code>, with the closest suggestion) in
				<code>meeko check</code>,
				<code>meeko build</code>
				and the editor; the build also rejects it with the file and the position.</li
			>
		</ul>

		<h2 id="types">Types</h2>
		<p
			>The module generates the union of the available names in
			<code>.meeko/types/modules/%40meekojs%2Ficon/</code>, one declaration per collection plus that of the local SVGs.
			<code>name</code>
			of
			<code>&lt;UIIcon&gt;</code>
			and
			<code>icon.include</code>
			of
			<code>meeko.config.ts</code>
			use that union. With Lucide (2,302 names), Tabler and Material Design Icons at once (22,802), TypeScript 7 checking grows by about 35 ms.</p
		>
		<DocsCode bind:code="examples.iconTypes" />

		<h2 id="local">Your own SVGs</h2>
		<p>
			Each file in
			<code>app/icons</code>
			(or in the folder of
			<code>icon.local</code>) is
			<code>local:&lt;name&gt;</code>, in kebab-case. It is sanitized at build time with a list of allowed elements and attributes:
			<code>&lt;foreignObject&gt;</code>,
			<code>&lt;image&gt;</code>, links, animations,
			<code>class</code>,
			<code>&lt;script&gt;</code>,
			<code>&lt;style&gt;</code>,
			<code>on*</code>
			handlers,
			<code>style</code>
			and every reference outside the icon (<code>href</code>
			and
			<code>url(…)</code>
			only to
			<code>#id</code>) are removed. Internal
			<code>id</code>s are prefixed per icon so two icons do not clash, and a single-color icon switches to
			<code>currentColor</code>.
		</p>
		<DocsPreview>
			<UIIcon
				name="local:paw"
				label="Meeko"
				size="1.5em"
			/>
		</DocsPreview>
		<DocsCode bind:code="examples.iconLocal" />

		<h2 id="accessibility">Accessibility, size and color</h2>
		<p>
			Without
			<code>label</code>, the icon is decorative:
			<code>aria-hidden="true"</code>
			and
			<code>focusable="false"</code>. With
			<code>label</code>, the
			<code>&lt;svg&gt;</code>
			gets
			<code>role="img"</code>
			and
			<code>aria-label</code>; use it when the icon is the only content of a button or a link.
		</p>
		<p>
			It is
			<code>1em</code>
			tall by default, so it follows the surrounding text, and it takes its color from
			<code>currentColor</code>. Width, height,
			<code>viewBox</code>
			and fill are attributes, never inline styles: it works with a strict Content Security Policy. The module's stylesheet only aligns the icon with the baseline (<code>.meeko-icon</code>).
		</p>
		<DocsPreview>
			<UIIcon
				name="lucide:star"
				size="16px"
			/>
			<UIIcon
				name="lucide:star"
				size="24px"
			/>
			<UIIcon
				name="lucide:star"
				size="2.5em"
			/>
		</DocsPreview>

		<h2 id="api">API</h2>
		<DocsApi
			bind:rows="[
				{ name: 'name', type: 'IconName', description: 'Icon: collection:icon or local:name. A literal is resolved at build time.' },
				{ name: 'label', type: 'string', description: 'Accessible text. Without it, the icon is decorative.' },
				{ name: 'size', type: 'string', default: 'icon.size (1em)', description: 'Height, a CSS length. The width follows the proportion of the icon.' },
				{ name: 'class', type: 'string', description: 'Classes of the svg, which always carries meeko-icon.' },
			]"
		/>
		<h3 id="options">Module options</h3>
		<DocsApi
			bind:rows="[
				{ name: 'collections', type: 'string[]', default: '[]', description: 'Iconify collections (lucide, mdi…); each one, the @iconify-json/<collection> package of the project. The editor completes the installed ones.' },
				{ name: 'include', type: 'IconInclude[]', default: '[]', description: 'Icons for dynamic names, or collection:* for a whole one.' },
				{ name: 'local', type: 'string | false', default: 'app/icons if it exists', description: 'Folder of your own SVGs.' },
				{ name: 'size', type: 'string', default: '1em', description: 'Default height of the icons.' },
			]"
		/>
	</DocsPage>
</template>
